        <div id="sizing-cadr-ach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Size an Air Purifier: CADR and ACH Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most important factor in whether an air purifier actually works for you, and it's also the one most shoppers get wrong. People tend to shop by the "coverage area" printed in bold on the box, buy a unit rated for a room roughly their size, and end up disappointed when it never seems to keep up. The problem isn't the purifier — it's that the coverage number alone doesn't tell you how thoroughly or how quickly it cleans that space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean Air Delivery Rate (CADR)</h3>
          <p>
            CADR measures how much filtered air a unit actually delivers per minute, tested separately for smoke, dust, and pollen particles. It's a far more useful number than the headline coverage area, because that coverage figure is usually calculated at the bare minimum air exchange rate manufacturers can get away with claiming. Two purifiers can both say "covers 400 sq ft" while one has nearly double the CADR of the other — and delivers a noticeably cleaner room as a result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Air Changes Per Hour (ACH)</h3>
          <p>
            ACH tells you how many times per hour a purifier fully cycles the air in a given room. Most manufacturer coverage claims are based on just 2 ACH, which is the bare minimum for general air freshening. For meaningful allergy or dust relief, aim for a unit that delivers <strong>4-5 ACH</strong> in your actual room size — not merely "rated" for it at the minimum standard. This is why sizing up, rather than matching a purifier's rating exactly to your square footage, makes such a noticeable difference in daily use.
          </p>

