        <div id="what-youre-filtering" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Are You Actually Filtering?
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Air purifier" covers a wide range of technology, and not every unit filters the same things. Before comparing specific models, it helps to know what each core filtration stage actually does — because the right combination depends entirely on what's bothering you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">True HEPA: Particulates — Dust, Pollen, Pet Dander</h3>
          <p>
            A true <strong>HEPA air purifier</strong> is independently certified to capture 99.97% of particles at 0.3 microns, which is considered the hardest particle size to trap. Dust, pollen, and pet dander are all larger than that threshold, so a genuine HEPA filter catches them even more reliably. This is the filtration stage doing the heavy lifting for anyone shopping specifically for an <strong>air purifier for allergies</strong>. Watch the wording carefully: "HEPA-type," "HEPA-like," and "99% HEPA" all describe filters that have not met the certified standard, and can let meaningfully more allergens through than the real thing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated Carbon: Odors, VOCs, and Smoke</h3>
          <p>
            HEPA filters trap solid particles, but they do essentially nothing for gases and odor — cooking smells, pet odor, off-gassing from new furniture, and wildfire smoke particulates that pass in gaseous form. An activated carbon layer adsorbs those compounds instead. Carbon layer thickness varies enormously between units: thin, cosmetic layers in budget purifiers saturate within weeks of regular use, while a dedicated carbon stage built for odor, like the ones in our wildfire smoke and whole-home picks below, holds up considerably longer under regular exposure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Combination Matters for Your Situation</h3>
          <p>
            For everyday allergies, dust, and pet dander, prioritize a strong true HEPA filter first — carbon is a nice-to-have but secondary. For wildfire smoke and persistent cooking or pet odor, you want both a strong HEPA stage and a genuinely substantial carbon layer, not the thin cosmetic version. Ionization technologies, sold under names like PlasmaWave, are a separate and optional add-on with mixed independent evidence behind them; every ionizer-equipped unit on this list can be switched off, and we'd treat it as a bonus feature rather than a reason to buy.
          </p>

