            The sticker price on an air purifier is only part of what it actually costs to own. Filters wear out, and replacement cost varies enormously between models — sometimes by a factor of three or more for units that otherwise perform similarly. Comparing two purifiers by upfront price alone, without checking what a year of filters costs, is one of the most common ways buyers end up disappointed with their choice months later.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electricity Is the Smaller Cost</h3>
          <p>
            Running a modern air purifier continuously typically costs somewhere between $10 and $30 per year in electricity, depending on the unit's efficiency and how often it's operating at higher fan speeds. This is a minor line item compared to filters, but it's worth noting that units running on auto mode, cycling between low and higher speeds as needed, tend to use less power over a year than units left on a fixed high setting around the clock.
          </p>

