                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">58-68 dB</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Comparable to a vacuum cleaner</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short bursts only — smoke events, not overnight</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            When shopping specifically for a <strong>quiet air purifier for bedroom</strong> use, check the manufacturer's dB rating on the lowest or dedicated sleep setting, not the average across all speeds — that number is what you'll actually be sleeping next to. Auto mode is worth relying on for the rest of the day, since it lets a unit run quieter most of the time while still ramping up briefly when it detects a real spike in particles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Whole House vs Room by Room -->
        <div id="whole-house-vs-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whole-House vs. Room-by-Room: Which Setup Do You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            One of the most common questions we get is whether to buy a single large <strong>whole house air purifier</strong> or several smaller units placed room by room. Both approaches work, but they suit different homes and budgets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When One Large Unit Makes Sense</h3>
          <p>
            An open floor plan where the kitchen, dining, and living areas share continuous airflow is the clearest case for a single large-capacity unit like the Winix 9800 or Coway Airmega 300S. Air moves relatively freely across the whole space, so one correctly sized purifier placed centrally can meaningfully improve the entire area, and it's simpler to maintain a single filter set rather than juggling several.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Multiple Smaller Units Make Sense</h3>
          <p>
            A home with distinct closed-off rooms — separate bedrooms, a home office behind a door, a nursery — usually benefits more from multiple smaller units like the Blueair Blue Pure 411i Max or Levoit Core 300S than one oversized purifier trying to reach every room through hallways and closed doors. Air doesn't flow well between rooms with closed doors, so a single central unit often ends up thoroughly cleaning only the room it's actually in.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">A Practical Middle Ground</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
              Many households do best with a hybrid approach: one larger unit for the main living area, plus a smaller, quieter unit dedicated to the primary bedroom for overnight use. This tends to cost less overall than trying to size a single massive purifier to reach every room in the house, and it lets you match noise level to each room's actual use.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air purifier for home use overall in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, the Coway Airmega 300S is the best all-around air purifier for home use — it combines true HEPA filtration, a real activated carbon stage, large-room coverage, and a genuinely useful smart app. If your primary concern is allergies specifically, the Honeywell HPA300 is a proven, lower-cost alternative. The right pick ultimately depends on your room size, budget, and whether noise or coverage matters more to you.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air purifier do I need for my room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the unit's Clean Air Delivery Rate (CADR) to your room's square footage, aiming for 4-5 air changes per hour rather than the minimum 2 ACH many manufacturers use for their headline coverage numbers. As a rough guide, a 300 sq ft bedroom needs roughly 200-250 CADR, a 500 sq ft living room needs roughly 330-415 CADR, and larger open floor plans need either a high-CADR whole house air purifier or multiple smaller units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a HEPA air purifier actually better for allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A true HEPA air purifier is certified to capture 99.97% of particles at 0.3 microns, which covers pollen, pet dander, dust mite debris, and mold fragments — the most common allergy triggers. Look specifically for the words true HEPA or HEPA H13 rather than HEPA-type or HEPA-like, which describe filters that have not met the certified standard and can let significantly more allergens through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run and maintain an air purifier per year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Electricity is minor — usually $10-30 per year running continuously, depending on fan speed and the unit's efficiency. Filter replacement is the real ongoing cost, typically running $30-90 per year depending on the model, with true HEPA filters generally replaced every 6-12 months and thinner carbon layers needing more frequent swaps. Always price out a full year of replacement filters before comparing sticker prices between models.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is too loud for a bedroom air purifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For overnight use, look for a unit that measures 25 dB or lower on its lowest or dedicated sleep setting — that's roughly the volume of quiet rustling leaves and sits below the threshold most people notice while falling asleep. Anything above about 35 dB on its lowest setting is likely to be audible enough to disrupt sleep for noise-sensitive users, so check the manufacturer's lowest-speed dB rating specifically, not just the average.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">My main concern is mold or musty odor, not allergies or dust. Is this still the right guide?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                This guide covers general everyday air quality — allergies, pet dander, dust, wildfire smoke, and everyday odor. If mold spores or a persistent musty smell in a basement or bathroom are your primary concern, see our dedicated <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">Best Air Purifiers for Mold</a> guide, which covers heavy-carbon models and mold-specific sizing that this guide doesn't focus on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes That Waste Money on a New Air Purifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Even a genuinely good purifier underperforms when it's undersized, mismanaged, or run the wrong way day to day. These are the mistakes we see homeowners make most often.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Undersizing CADR for the Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying a purifier rated for roughly your room's square footage, rather than sizing up for a real 4-5 ACH air exchange, is the single most common reason people feel like their purifier "isn't doing anything." Manufacturer coverage numbers are typically calculated at the bare minimum 2 ACH standard, which is optimistic for meaningful allergy relief or fast odor clearing. When in doubt, size up rather than matching a unit exactly to your room.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Filter Replacement Cost When Comparing Sticker Price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheaper purifier with expensive, frequently replaced filters can easily cost more over a few years than a pricier unit with efficient, long-lasting filters. Always check the annual filter cost and replacement frequency for a specific model before comparing upfront prices between purifiers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It on the Lowest Speed 24/7</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Keeping a purifier on its quietest setting around the clock feels efficient, but it dramatically under-filters a room during actual particle spikes — cooking, guests, pets, or high pollen days. Auto mode, which most mid-range and premium units include, is almost always the better choice: it runs quiet most of the time but ramps up automatically when it actually needs to.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Placing the Unit in a Corner That Blocks Airflow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tucking a purifier into a tight corner, behind furniture, or against a wall restricts the intake and exhaust airflow it needs to actually circulate air through a room. Keep at least a foot of clearance on all sides, and place it somewhere with reasonably open airflow toward the center of the room rather than the most out-of-the-way spot available.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

